# SvelteKit 2 + Svelte 5 (Runes) Anti-Patterns

Cursor: when generating Svelte or SvelteKit code, refuse to emit any of the patterns below. Each entry has the BAD form, the GOOD replacement, and a one-line explanation. Target: `svelte ^5.55.7`, `@sveltejs/kit ^2.60.1`, `vite ^8.0.13`, `typescript ^6.0.3`, `vitest ^4.1.6`, `@playwright/test ^1.60.0`, `tailwindcss ^4.3.0`, `bits-ui ^2.18.1`, `@lucide/svelte ^1.16.0`, `sveltekit-superforms ^2.30.1`, `formsnap ^2.0.1`, `zod ^4.4.3`. Node 24 LTS.

The companion `svelte-5-runes`, `svelte-5-events-and-snippets`, `sveltekit-2-core`, `sveltekit-routing-and-load`, `sveltekit-forms-and-actions`, `sveltekit-app-state`, `svelte-5-reactivity-traps`, `svelte-5-typescript`, and `sveltekit-testing` rules cover the matching positive patterns.

## Section A: Svelte 5 syntax

### 1. `let count = 0` for component state without `$state(0)`

In runes mode (Svelte 5 default for any project that opts in or any `.svelte.ts` module), bare `let` declarations are NOT reactive. Mutating them does not re-render. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$state

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let count = 0
  const inc = () => count++  // mutates the local, no re-render
</script>
<button onclick={inc}>{count}</button>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  const inc = () => count++
</script>
<button onclick={inc}>{count}</button>
```

### 2. `$:` reactive statements in runes-mode files

`$:` is the Svelte 4 / non-runes reactive statement. In runes mode it is a syntax error. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$derived

```svelte
<!-- BAD (runes mode) -->
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  $: doubled = count * 2
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  let doubled = $derived(count * 2)
</script>
```

### 3. `export let prop` in any file using runes

`export let` is the Svelte 4 prop declaration. Runes mode requires `$props()`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$props

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  export let title: string
  export let count = 0
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { title, count = 0 }: { title: string; count?: number } = $props()
</script>
```

### 4. `on:click` and any `on:event` directive

Svelte 5 dropped the `on:` namespace. Event handlers are plain DOM attributes, the same as React / vanilla HTML. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/svelte-5-migration-guide#Event-changes

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<button on:click={handle}>Click</button>
<input on:input={oninput} />
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<button onclick={handle}>Click</button>
<input oninput={oninput} />
```

### 5. Event modifiers `|preventDefault`, `|stopPropagation`, `|self`, `|once`

Event modifiers were removed with the `on:` namespace. Handle the modifier in the function body. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/svelte-5-migration-guide#Event-changes-Event-modifiers

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<form on:submit|preventDefault={save}>...</form>
<a on:click|stopPropagation={openDialog}>...</a>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<form onsubmit={(e) => { e.preventDefault(); save(e) }}>...</form>
<a onclick={(e) => { e.stopPropagation(); openDialog() }}>...</a>
```

### 6. `createEventDispatcher` + `dispatch('foo', payload)`

`createEventDispatcher` is gone. Components communicate via callback props. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/svelte-5-migration-guide#Event-changes-Component-events

```svelte
<!-- BAD - child -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { createEventDispatcher } from 'svelte'
  const dispatch = createEventDispatcher<{ select: { id: string } }>()
  const choose = (id: string) => dispatch('select', { id })
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - child -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { onselect }: { onselect: (id: string) => void } = $props()
  const choose = (id: string) => onselect(id)
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - parent -->
<Picker onselect={(id) => console.log(id)} />
```

### 7. `<slot />` and `<slot name="x" />`

Slots were replaced by snippets in Svelte 5. The default slot is `children`; named slots are individual snippet props. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/snippet

```svelte
<!-- BAD - child -->
<div class="card">
  <slot name="header" />
  <slot />
</div>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - child -->
<script lang="ts">
  import type { Snippet } from 'svelte'
  let { header, children }: { header?: Snippet; children?: Snippet } = $props()
</script>
<div class="card">
  {@render header?.()}
  {@render children?.()}
</div>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - parent -->
<Card>
  {#snippet header()}<h2>Title</h2>{/snippet}
  <p>Body content.</p>
</Card>
```

### 8. Component instantiation via `new App({ target })`

Svelte 5 dropped the class instance API. Use `mount()` for client roots, `hydrate()` for SSR'd roots. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/svelte-5-migration-guide#Components-are-no-longer-classes

```ts
// BAD - main.ts
import App from './App.svelte'
const app = new App({ target: document.getElementById('app')! })
export default app
```

```ts
// GOOD - main.ts
import { mount } from 'svelte'
import App from './App.svelte'
const app = mount(App, { target: document.getElementById('app')! })
export default app
```

### 9. `bind:this={el}` to a non-`$state` variable

`bind:this` writes to its target. In runes mode the target must be a `$state` ref so the assignment triggers reactivity. A bare `let el` will receive the node but no consumer of `el` will re-run. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/bind#bind-this

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let el: HTMLDivElement  // bare let; reads of el do not re-run
  $effect(() => { console.log(el?.offsetWidth) })  // never sees the assignment
</script>
<div bind:this={el}>...</div>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let el = $state<HTMLDivElement | undefined>()
  $effect(() => { if (el) console.log(el.offsetWidth) })
</script>
<div bind:this={el}>...</div>
```

### 10. Destructuring a `$state` proxy and using the destructured local

`$state` returns a proxy. Destructuring captures the value at that moment; the local is no longer reactive. Read through the proxy. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$state#Deep-state

```ts
// BAD
const cart = $state({ items: [], total: 0 })
const { items } = cart  // local snapshot
function add(i: Item) { items.push(i) }  // mutates the snapshot, but consumers reading cart.items also see it because it's the same array - until cart is reassigned
```

```ts
// GOOD
const cart = $state({ items: [] as Item[], total: 0 })
function add(i: Item) { cart.items.push(i) }  // read through the proxy
```

## Section B: Reactivity

### 11. Setting `$state` inside `$effect` to derive a value

If the new value is a pure function of other state, it is a `$derived`, not a side effect. `$effect` for derivation is the most common Svelte 5 LLM regression. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$effect#When-not-to-use-effects

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  let doubled = $state(0)
  $effect(() => { doubled = count * 2 })
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
  let doubled = $derived(count * 2)
</script>
```

### 12. `$effect` reading and writing the same `$state` (infinite loop)

`$effect` re-runs whenever any read state changes. Writing to a state it reads creates an infinite loop. If you genuinely need a write that does not retrigger, wrap reads in `untrack`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$effect#untrack

```svelte
<!-- BAD - infinite loop -->
<script lang="ts">
  let value = $state(0)
  $effect(() => { value = value + 1 })  // reads value, writes value, repeats
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { untrack } from 'svelte'
  let value = $state(0)
  let trigger = $state(0)
  $effect(() => {
    trigger  // tracked
    value = untrack(() => value) + 1
  })
</script>
```

### 13. `$state` for arrays larger than 1000 elements without considering `$state.raw`

`$state` wraps every nested object / array in a Proxy. For large data sets the proxy overhead dominates render time. `$state.raw` opts out of deep proxying; mutate by replacement. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$state#$state.raw

```ts
// BAD - 50,000 row table
let rows = $state<Row[]>(loadFiftyThousandRows())  // each row proxied
```

```ts
// GOOD
let rows = $state.raw<Row[]>(loadFiftyThousandRows())
function addRow(r: Row) { rows = [...rows, r] }  // replace, do not push
```

### 14. `$effect` used to initialise state on the server

Effects only run in the browser. Initialisation in `$effect` produces undefined SSR output and a hydration mismatch. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$effect#Effects-only-run-in-the-browser

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let now = $state<string>('')
  $effect(() => { now = new Date().toISOString() })  // empty string in SSR HTML
</script>
<p>{now}</p>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - if you really need it on both, derive from a load value -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { now }: { now: string } = $props()  // passed from +page.ts load
</script>
<p>{now}</p>
```

### 15. Reading state inside `$effect` after `await` (no longer tracked)

`$effect` only tracks state read synchronously before the first `await`. Reads after the await are not tracked, so the effect will not re-run when they change. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$effect#Understanding-dependencies

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let id = $state('a')
  let other = $state('x')
  $effect(async () => {
    await fetch(`/api/${id}`)  // tracks id
    console.log(other)         // does NOT track other
  })
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - read everything you need to track up front -->
<script lang="ts">
  let id = $state('a')
  let other = $state('x')
  $effect(() => {
    const i = id
    const o = other  // tracked synchronously
    void (async () => {
      await fetch(`/api/${i}`)
      console.log(o)
    })()
  })
</script>
```

### 16. Importing from `svelte/legacy` in new code

`svelte/legacy` (`run`, `createEventDispatcher`, `createBubbler`, `nonpassive`) exists for migration tooling output. New code should reach for runes / callback props instead. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/legacy-overview

```ts
// BAD - new code
import { run, createEventDispatcher } from 'svelte/legacy'
```

```ts
// GOOD - use $effect / callback props
// (no import needed; runes are language-level)
```

## Section C: SvelteKit 2 routing

### 17. `throw error(...)` / `throw redirect(...)` in SvelteKit 2

SvelteKit 2 changed `error()` and `redirect()` to throw internally. You no longer prepend `throw`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#redirect-and-error-are-no-longer-thrown-by-you

```ts
// BAD
import { error, redirect } from '@sveltejs/kit'
export const load = async ({ params }) => {
  if (!params.id) throw error(400, 'Missing id')
  if (!user) throw redirect(303, '/login')
}
```

```ts
// GOOD
import { error, redirect } from '@sveltejs/kit'
export const load = async ({ params }) => {
  if (!params.id) error(400, 'Missing id')
  if (!user) redirect(303, '/login')
}
```

### 18. Wrapping `redirect()` in try/catch (swallows the redirect)

Because `redirect()` throws internally, a catch-all `try { ... } catch { ... }` will swallow it. Re-throw or use `isHttpError` / `isRedirect` to filter. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#redirect-and-error-are-no-longer-thrown-by-you

```ts
// BAD
import { redirect, isRedirect } from '@sveltejs/kit'
export const load = async () => {
  try {
    if (!user) redirect(303, '/login')  // throws Redirect
    return { user }
  } catch (e) {
    return { user: null }  // swallows the redirect
  }
}
```

```ts
// GOOD
import { redirect, isRedirect } from '@sveltejs/kit'
export const load = async () => {
  try {
    if (!user) redirect(303, '/login')
    return { user }
  } catch (e) {
    if (isRedirect(e)) throw e  // re-throw redirects
    return { user: null }
  }
}
```

### 19. `cookies.set` / `cookies.delete` without explicit `path: '/'`

SvelteKit 2 made `path` mandatory on cookie operations. There is no longer a default. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#path-is-required-when-setting-cookies

```ts
// BAD
cookies.set('session', token, { httpOnly: true })  // throws in SvelteKit 2
cookies.delete('session')
```

```ts
// GOOD
cookies.set('session', token, { path: '/', httpOnly: true })
cookies.delete('session', { path: '/' })
```

### 20. Top-level unawaited promises in `load`

SvelteKit 1 auto-awaited top-level promises returned from `load`. SvelteKit 2 requires explicit `await` (or `Promise.all`) for type safety and clarity. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#top-level-promises-are-no-longer-awaited

```ts
// BAD - SvelteKit 2
export const load = async ({ fetch }) => {
  return {
    posts: fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json()),  // not auto-awaited; type is Promise<unknown>
    tags: fetch('/api/tags').then(r => r.json()),
  }
}
```

```ts
// GOOD - explicit Promise.all for parallel fetches
export const load = async ({ fetch }) => {
  const [posts, tags] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('/api/tags').then(r => r.json()),
  ])
  return { posts, tags }
}
```

### 21. `goto('https://external')`

SvelteKit 2 rejects external URLs in `goto()`. Use `window.location.href` for full-page navigations to other origins. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#goto-no-longer-accepts-external-urls

```ts
// BAD
import { goto } from '$app/navigation'
goto('https://example.com')  // throws in SvelteKit 2
```

```ts
// GOOD
window.location.href = 'https://example.com'
```

### 22. `$page` from `$app/stores` in new code

`$app/stores` was deprecated in `@sveltejs/kit` 2.12 in favour of `$app/state`, which exposes plain reactive objects (no `$` prefix, no store subscription). Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/$app-state

```svelte
<!-- BAD - new code on Kit 2.12+ -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { page } from '$app/stores'
</script>
<p>{$page.url.pathname}</p>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { page } from '$app/state'
</script>
<p>{page.url.pathname}</p>
```

### 23. `resolvePath` (renamed `resolveRoute`)

`resolvePath` was renamed `resolveRoute` in `$app/paths`. The old name is removed in SvelteKit 2. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#resolvePath-is-replaced-by-resolveRoute

```ts
// BAD
import { resolvePath } from '$app/paths'
const href = resolvePath('/posts/[slug]', { slug: 'hello' })
```

```ts
// GOOD
import { resolveRoute } from '$app/paths'
const href = resolveRoute('/posts/[slug]', { slug: 'hello' })
```

## Section D: SvelteKit 2 forms

### 24. `use:enhance` callback args `form` / `data` (renamed `formElement` / `formData`)

In SvelteKit 2 the `enhance` callback gives you `formElement` and `formData` instead of the ambiguous `form` and `data`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#use-enhance-callback-changes

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<form
  method="POST"
  use:enhance={({ form, data, action, cancel }) => {
    return async ({ result }) => {}
  }}
>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<form
  method="POST"
  use:enhance={({ formElement, formData, action, cancel }) => {
    return async ({ result }) => {}
  }}
>
```

### 25. Form with file inputs missing `enctype="multipart/form-data"`

SvelteKit 2 throws under `use:enhance` if a form contains `<input type="file">` and the `<form>` does not have `enctype="multipart/form-data"`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#forms-with-file-inputs-must-use-multipart-form-data

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<form method="POST" use:enhance>
  <input type="file" name="avatar" />
  <button>Upload</button>
</form>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<form method="POST" enctype="multipart/form-data" use:enhance>
  <input type="file" name="avatar" />
  <button>Upload</button>
</form>
```

### 26. `paths.relative: undefined` (default flipped to `true`)

SvelteKit 2 flipped the default of `kit.paths.relative` from `false` to `true`. If your code hardcodes `paths.relative` knowledge, declare the value explicitly to avoid surprises. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-2#paths-relative-default-changed

```ts
// BAD - assumes the v1 default
// svelte.config.js (paths key omitted)
```

```ts
// GOOD
// svelte.config.js
const config = {
  kit: {
    paths: { relative: true },  // declared, matches the v2 default
  },
}
export default config
```

### 27. `$env/dynamic/*` read during prerender

Prerender runs at build time; dynamic env vars (read at runtime) are not available. Use `$env/static/*` for build-time-known constants. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/$env-dynamic-private

```ts
// BAD - +page.server.ts with prerender = true
import { env } from '$env/dynamic/private'
export const prerender = true
export const load = async () => {
  return { apiKey: env.API_KEY }  // undefined at prerender
}
```

```ts
// GOOD
import { API_KEY } from '$env/static/private'
export const prerender = true
export const load = async () => {
  return { apiKey: API_KEY }
}
```

## Section E: state modules and stores

### 28. `writable()` / `readable()` stores for component-local state

Stores were the Svelte 4 reactivity mechanism for component-local state. Svelte 5 uses `$state`. Stores still work but are no longer the documented shape for new component code. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/stores

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { writable } from 'svelte/store'
  const count = writable(0)
</script>
<button onclick={() => count.update(n => n + 1)}>{$count}</button>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
</script>
<button onclick={() => count++}>{count}</button>
```

### 29. Stores in `.ts` modules for cross-component shared state

For shared state across components, use a `.svelte.ts` module that exports `$state`. The `.svelte.ts` extension tells the compiler to treat the module as runes-aware. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$state#Universal-reactivity

```ts
// BAD - src/lib/cart.ts
import { writable } from 'svelte/store'
export const cart = writable<{ items: Item[] }>({ items: [] })
```

```ts
// GOOD - src/lib/state/cart.svelte.ts
class Cart {
  items = $state<Item[]>([])
  get total() { return this.items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0) }
  add(i: Item) { this.items.push(i) }
}
export const cart = new Cart()
```

### 30. `lucide-svelte` import (use `@lucide/svelte`)

`lucide-svelte` was the Svelte 4 package; `@lucide/svelte` is the scoped Svelte 5 fork that ships native runes support. Source: https://lucide.dev/guide/packages/lucide-svelte

```ts
// BAD
import { Camera } from 'lucide-svelte'
```

```ts
// GOOD
import Camera from '@lucide/svelte/icons/camera'
```

### 31. `melt-ui` import (use `bits-ui` for Svelte 5)

`@melt-ui/svelte` was the headless library for Svelte 4. For Svelte 5 the documented headless layer is `bits-ui` (the layer underneath `shadcn-svelte`). `@melt-ui/svelte` is in maintenance only. Source: https://www.bits-ui.com

```ts
// BAD - new Svelte 5 code
import { createDialog } from '@melt-ui/svelte'
```

```ts
// GOOD
import { Dialog } from 'bits-ui'
```

### 32. Felte for forms

Felte (`felte`, `@felte/validator-zod`) is effectively abandoned for SvelteKit. The documented forms stack is `sveltekit-superforms` + `formsnap` + `zod`. Source: https://superforms.rocks

```ts
// BAD
import { createForm } from 'felte'
import { validator } from '@felte/validator-zod'
```

```ts
// GOOD - +page.server.ts
import { superValidate } from 'sveltekit-superforms'
import { zod } from 'sveltekit-superforms/adapters'
import { schema } from './schema'
export const load = async () => ({ form: await superValidate(zod(schema)) })
```

### 33. `<img>` for above-the-fold images

`@sveltejs/enhanced-img` rewrites `<enhanced:img>` at build time to a `<picture>` with AVIF / WebP sources, intrinsic dimensions, and srcset. Plain `<img>` ships the original asset and triggers CLS. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/images

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<img src="/hero.jpg" alt="Hero" />
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<enhanced:img src="./hero.jpg?enhanced" alt="Hero" />
```

## Section F: Svelte 5 typing

### 34. Component event payload typed via `CustomEvent<T>`

`CustomEvent<T>` was the Svelte 4 typing for `createEventDispatcher` payloads. With callback props the payload is the function signature, no `CustomEvent` wrapper. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/svelte-5-migration-guide#Event-changes

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  function onSelect(e: CustomEvent<{ id: string }>) { console.log(e.detail.id) }
</script>
<Picker on:select={onSelect} />
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  function onSelect(id: string) { console.log(id) }
</script>
<Picker onselect={onSelect} />
```

### 35. `withDefaults`-style prop defaults

There is no `withDefaults` in Svelte 5. Defaults work as native JavaScript destructure defaults inside `$props()`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$props

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  type Props = { size?: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg' }
  const { size }: Props = withDefaults($props<Props>(), { size: 'md' })
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { size = 'md' }: { size?: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg' } = $props()
</script>
```

### 36. Manually generating SSR-safe IDs for ARIA

Svelte 5.20 added `$props.id()` for stable, SSR-safe component instance IDs. Use it for `aria-labelledby`, `aria-describedby`, `for`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$props#$props.id

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  const uid = `field-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2)}`  // SSR + client mismatch
</script>
<label for={uid}>Email</label>
<input id={uid} type="email" />
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  const uid = $props.id()
</script>
<label for="{uid}-email">Email</label>
<input id="{uid}-email" type="email" />
```

### 37. `let { children } = $props()` then `<slot>`

If you destructure `children` from `$props()`, render with `{@render children?.()}`, NOT `<slot>`. The two systems do not co-exist. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/snippet#Default-content

```svelte
<!-- BAD -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { children } = $props()
</script>
<div><slot /></div>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD -->
<script lang="ts">
  import type { Snippet } from 'svelte'
  let { children }: { children?: Snippet } = $props()
</script>
<div>{@render children?.()}</div>
```

### 38. Mutating non-`$bindable` props in a child

Props are read-only in the child by default. Mutating them throws in dev. To allow two-way binding, mark the prop `$bindable()`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$bindable

```svelte
<!-- BAD - child -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { value } = $props()
  function reset() { value = '' }  // throws in dev
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - child -->
<script lang="ts">
  let { value = $bindable('') }: { value?: string } = $props()
  function reset() { value = '' }
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- GOOD - parent -->
<TextInput bind:value={text} />
```

## Section G: testing and types

### 39. `+page.ts` exports without `satisfies PageLoad`

Type the `load` export with `satisfies PageLoad` so Svelte's generated `$types` propagates the return shape into `+page.svelte`. Source: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/load#Type-safety

```ts
// BAD - src/routes/posts/[slug]/+page.ts
export const load = async ({ params }) => {
  return { slug: params.slug }
}
```

```ts
// GOOD
import type { PageLoad } from './$types'
export const load: PageLoad = async ({ params }) => {
  return { slug: params.slug }
}
// or:
// export const load = (async ({ params }) => ({ slug: params.slug })) satisfies PageLoad
```

### 40. `mount(App)` without unmount on teardown / `new App()` for tests

Use `@testing-library/svelte`'s `render()` for component tests. Manual `mount()` requires manual `unmount()` and bypasses `cleanup()`. `new App({ target })` is the dropped Svelte 4 instance API. Source: https://testing-library.com/docs/svelte-testing-library/intro

```ts
// BAD - Counter.test.ts
import { mount } from 'svelte'
import Counter from './Counter.svelte'
test('renders', () => {
  const target = document.createElement('div')
  mount(Counter, { target })
  expect(target.textContent).toContain('0')
  // no unmount; handlers leak across tests
})
```

```ts
// GOOD
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/svelte'
import { test, expect } from 'vitest'
import Counter from './Counter.svelte'
test('renders', () => {
  render(Counter)
  expect(screen.getByRole('button')).toHaveTextContent('0')
})
```
